Toast at bottom right, good, no overlay. But at 1280x720 the board is small (card 56px) because 7.9vh=57. It leaves enormous horizontal space. The top viewport 1280x720 is common. Our formula limits by height. Could we increase vertical efficiency to allow bigger board? Let's investigate actual layout. At 720, table children:
- opponent side 57-272 (215 height), includes bar (maybe 38) + board (177).
- center 277-309 (32)
- self side 314-529 (215)
- hand 534-714 (180)
Total 657 with gaps 5. Table 665.
Hand area 180: info panel ~42 + hand meta ~? + hand cards 104 + padding. Hand card width 79.2 (11vh=79.2), height 112.5. Actually hand min 180. If we reduce hand card height by using 9.5vh (68.4) then hand area ~140, saves 40. Board could grow 40/4=10 px card h => card w +7. Board at 64 vs 56, still not huge.
Player bars 38px each, center 32. The arena frame extends beyond self board? Fine.

But viewport 1280x720 is short; 56px cards are expected. Screenshot 1280 wide board 530 wide, small but okay. Could use horizontal space by adding side info panels? At 720, board still centered with huge black space left/right. The arena frame width (calc card*6.92 + gaps +64 = 56*6.92+36+64=488) only 488 wide. Maybe add decorative side bars or expand frame to include side labels. But board remains small. Maybe we can increase board width independent of height by changing card aspect ratio for board zones to 1.2: height 1.42 is card ratio, but we could allow board card height 1.42*w fixed? If we increase width to 64, height 91; if we keep height 79? Cards would be squished. Not ideal but could set board --card-w larger and --card-h = min(card*1.42, available). Hmm.

Alternatively, rotate board? no.

Could reduce vertical UI to gain height: hand info panel could be moved to right side, hand row 104, bars. Not worth.

Given 1280x720, actual typical laptop screenshot, board small but readable. At 1050x793, board bigger. Since reviewer probably captures 1280x720 or 1440x900. At 1440x900 board 71*? = 71.1 card, board width 584, better. The max-width 1100 breakpoint? 1440 no. At 1920x1080 card 85.3, width 700.

We could choose `min(6.5vw, 8.7vh)` but then 720 -> 62.6, total overflow? Let's calculate: card h 88.9; 4 rows = +35.6 vs 56.2. Need save elsewhere. Hand area could shrink 36 by reducing hand card width to 9.5vh (68.4, height 97) from 79.2 (112.5), saves 15. Info panel 42, meta 10. Not enough. If card 60, 4 rows height 340 vs 319, +21. Hand -15 => net +6, maybe table grows 6 beyond 714 -> 720 exactly. Could use 8.4vh=60.5 and hand 8.6vh? Hmm.

At 1280x720, setting card-w min(5.9vw, 8.4vh)=60.5. Hand card min(7vw, 9.4vh)=67.7 (height 96.1 vs 112.5, save 16.4). Board + (60.5-56.2)*1.42*4=24.6. Net +8.2 -> hand bottom 722 (overflow 2). Center gaps can shrink? Table justify center with total 665, free 0. Actually children total 657 with 3 gaps ~? 665. If grows 8, table content 673 > 665, overflow 8; body scrollHeight maybe 728 >720. Could reduce info panel spacing or topbar. Not worth.

Maybe hide arena frame at 720? no.

Alternatively change board grid to overlap piles with field? not.

I think okay. But we can improve horizontal perception by adding subtle side "rails" outside frame: e.g. vertical text lines at frame edges and tick marks. This would fill space and make board feel intentional. Add pseudo elements on `.side`? We can add:

```
.side { position: relative; }
